fix: see deletes inside interpreter one-liners

Round 4 found no regression, one pre-existing gap, and one detail of the
same family that the report flagged in passing.

Interpreter wrappers were not unwrapped. `python -c 'import shutil;
shutil.rmtree("/tmp/x")'`, `node -e 'require("fs").rmSync(...)'` and
`perl -e 'unlink ...'` all passed, because the script body is glued into a
single token by tokenize. The delete verbs here are statically visible -
this is not the documented "dynamically constructed command" limitation -
so they belong in the unwrap table alongside cmd /c and sh -c. python,
python3, node, perl, ruby, php and lua with -c/-e/-E/-r now unwrap into the
normal nested parse. Running a script file is still allowed: its contents
are not statically known.

The single-quote mask treated every quote as a string delimiter, so
`echo "'$(rm -rf x)'"` was read as a single-quoted range and the
substitution was missed. That inner quote sits inside double quotes, where
POSIX treats it as an ordinary character and still expands `$()`. The mask
now tracks both quote states and only opens a single-quoted range outside
double quotes.

Mutation checks confirm both tests bite: disabling the interpreter unwrap,
or reverting the mask to a naive toggle, turns the suite red.

267 tests, 28 files.
